<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="runtime-weaving" title="Runtime weaving">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Needs the API and the processor from <a href="install.topic">Install</a>. Keep the roots
            you weave off your application classpath.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        Your own code builds the loader, hands it the roots and closes it. This is how a plugin
        host loads jars it did not compile against, not how you write a <code>WeaverPlugin</code>
        itself. No agent, no flag, and nothing on disk is rewritten.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="java" src="Launcher.java"/>

    <p>
        <code>ConfigParser.ofSystemProperties</code> reads <code>aether.weaver.*</code> properties
        into that config, so the deflist below sets each key with <code>-D</code> on the command
        line.
    </p>

    <chapter title="What happens per class" id="delegation">
        <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["loadClass on your weaving loader"] --> B{"Can the parent reach it?"}
    B -->|yes| C["Parent defines it, unwoven, silently"]
    B -->|no| D["Bytes read from the roots you gave"]
    D --> E["Weaver rewrites them"]
    E --> F["Defined here, woven"]
        </code-block>

        <p>
            Only what this loader defines itself is rewritten. Behind it sits the engine every
            driver shares, which <a href="pipeline.topic">The weaving pipeline</a> walks through.
        </p>

        <warning>
            <p>
                Delegation itself reports nothing. Your target runs unchanged and no diagnostic
                points at it, so a root whose classes the parent can already reach — without a
                manifest of its own — looks exactly like a weave that matched nothing. Give the
                loader roots nothing above it can see.
            </p>
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <procedure title="Run it and check it" id="run">
        <step>
            <p>Compile the target and the weave into a directory of their own, with the API and
                the processor on the path. The processor writes the manifest the loader finds them
                through, and <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> follows it.
                <a href="first-weave.topic">Your first weave</a> is these two classes.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash"><![CDATA[
javac -proc:full -cp "lib/*" \
      -d plugins src/fixture/Target.java src/fixture/Audit.java
]]></code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Add <code>aether-weaver-runtime</code> to <code>lib</code>. It brings the engine
                and the API with it, and the launcher needs nothing else.
                <a href="artifacts.topic">Artefacts and modules</a> has the coordinates.</p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Run, with <code>plugins</code> left out of that classpath.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash"><![CDATA[
java -cp "classes:lib/*" launcher.Launcher
]]></code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Three lines, and the middle one is the proof.</p>
            <code-block lang="console"><![CDATA[
WeavingClassLoader[roots=1, fingerprint=<64 hex characters>]
woven
hello
]]></code-block>
            <p><code>hello</code> is what <code>Target.greet()</code> returns on its own.
                <code>woven</code> comes from the handler, so a load that reached an unwoven class
                prints <code>hello</code> by itself.</p>
        </step>
    </procedure>

    <chapter title="When a class loads unwoven anyway" id="unwoven">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="Its bytes came from a signed jar">
                <p>
                    Reported as <code>AW3002</code>, and the original bytes are defined. Sign after
                    weaving, or set <code>-Daether.weaver.policy.allowSigned=true</code> and accept
                    a signature that no longer describes what runs.
                    <a href="diagnostics-policy.topic">AW3xxx</a> has the gate.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Weaving it threw">
                <p>
                    Reported as <code>AW4090</code> for a <code>RuntimeException</code> or a
                    <code>LinkageError</code>; the load then fails with
                    <code>ClassNotFoundException</code>. Any other <code>Error</code> propagates
                    unchanged. Set <code>-Daether.weaver.onError=report</code> to define the
                    original bytes and carry on instead.
                    <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Configuration keys</a> has both values.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The JVM was started with an AOT cache flag">
                <p>
                    Reported as <code>AW2401</code>, once when you construct the loader rather than
                    once per class. Weave at build time instead, and the two stop competing:
                    <a href="build-time-weaving.topic">Build-time weaving</a> binds the goals,
                    <a href="diagnostics-environment.topic">AW2xxx</a> names the three flags.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            A class the roots already carry woven, by this same plan, is skipped: nothing runs
            twice. A second, different plan weaves it again and reports <code>AW2202</code>.
            <a href="choose-a-driver.topic#both">Running two of them</a> has the three cases.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Keeping the report for yourself" id="report">
        <p>
            <code>create</code> builds the weaver and keeps it to itself. An explain report it
            built reaches nobody. Build the weaver yourself and pass it to the public constructor.
            The report is then yours to print.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="java" src="Explained.java"/>

        <p>
            That constructor reads no manifest, so what you hand it is exactly what it weaves.
            <code>Explained.java</code> above discovers no <code>WeaverPlugin</code> and skips
            extensions. Only the Maven plugin gives the engine either, and
            <a href="extension-methods.topic">Extension methods</a> says why.
            <a href="diagnosing-a-weave.topic">Diagnosing a weave</a> reads a report line by line.
        </p>
    </chapter>

